# --- registry -------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by setup/lib/models.sh; passed to llama-server as environment and
# ignored there. MODEL_SWA=yes means the profile MUST carry --swa-full —
# without it every Claude Code turn re-processes the whole prompt.
MODEL_TITLE=Fast sidekick · 13.5 GiB · MoE 128/8 active · QAT-q4_0
MODEL_SWA=yes
# MODEL_SOURCE — where this model comes from, so the profile can fetch it.
# `<repo> <pattern>…`: every pattern is a substring of a path in that repo and
# every matching .gguf is fetched, so a sharded quant comes down whole and an
# mmproj rides along. Read by setup/get-model.sh, which is this stack's
# equivalent of `ollama pull` — except that the list it offers is MEASURED on
# this hardware (bash setup/lib/models.sh table) rather than a generic catalog.
# Verified 26.08. by matching the exact filename in the repo, not by guessing.
MODEL_SOURCE=google/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-qat-q4_0-gguf gemma-4-26B_q4_0-it gemma-4-26B-it-mmproj
# MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN — measured. Read by setup/lib/budget.py.
MODEL_KV_KIB_PER_TOKEN=10.4
MODEL_KV_SOURCE=this profile's own note below: 1.3 GiB at -c 131072 thanks to the sliding window (210 KiB/token on the 5 global layers of 30; the other 25 hold only a 1024 window). 1.3 GiB over 131,072 cells is 10.4 KiB per token.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fast sidekick · 13.5 GiB · MoE 128/8 active · QAT-q4_0
# SWA 25/30 layers, window 1024 -> KV extremely cheap (2.6 GiB @262k)
# -ctxcp/-cms because of SWA: otherwise llama.cpp discards the prompt cache
# -c 131072: thanks to the sliding window, KV costs only 1.3 GiB at 131k — the
#   large context is practically free here (measured 210 KiB/token on only the
#   5 global of 30 layers, the rest hold just a 1024 window).
# -cram WAS 32768, and it bought 24.6 full windows against the 2.2 that
# production runs on (`budget.py --cache`). The sliding window makes KV only
# 1.3 GiB at -c 131072, so the copied value was eight times more cache than
# this model can use in any plausible session.
#
# It was left alone earlier the same day, on the argument that at 128 GB it
# costs nothing that is needed — 59 GiB spare either way. That argument was
# about ONE machine. Strix Halo also ships with 64 GB, and there this single
# copied number is the whole reason the profile does not fit:
#
#     -cram 32768   65.9 of 62 GiB   does not fit
#     -cram  4096   37.8 of 62 GiB   fits, 3.1 windows of cache
#
# Corrected, and the reason is not portability: 4096 is the RIGHT number here
# and 32768 was never computed. That it also lets a smaller machine run this
# profile is a consequence, not the argument.
LLAMA_ARGS=--alias gemma26 -m @MODELS@/gemma-4-26B_q4_0-it.gguf --mmproj @MODELS@/gemma-4-26B-it-mmproj.gguf \
  -ngl 999 -fa on -ub 512 -b 2048 -c 131072 -ctk q8_0 -ctv q8_0 \
  --kv-unified -cram 4096 -ctxcp 64 -cms 4096 \
  --jinja --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8081

# Backend for this profile. Measured 24.08. (docs/MODELS.md): the choice
# is MODEL-dependent — Vulkan wins for models without speculation, ROCm wins
# 2x once MTP/ngram speculation is on. Inert until llama-user@.service is
# switched to: ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'exec "$HOME/$LLAMA_BIN" $LLAMA_ARGS' (one sudo install).
LLAMA_BIN=llama.cpp/build-vulkan/bin/llama-server
